--  Records and arrays: saying that several things are one thing, and that one
--  thing happens many times.
--
--  A shell script that tracked a number and a piece of text together had to
--  carry two variables and keep them in step by hand, and a subprogram taking
--  both had to take two arguments and trust the caller. A script that wanted
--  ten of something had to write ten variables or squeeze them into one String
--  with a separator nobody agreed on.

--  A record groups what belongs together. The components have their own types.
type Reading is record
   Label : String;
   Value : Integer;
end record;

--  An aggregate builds one. Positionally, or by naming the components, which
--  lets a reader see which value is which without counting.
First  : Reading := ("load", 42);
Second : Reading := (Value => 7, Label => "queue");

put_line (First.Label & " = " & Trim (First.Value'Image));
put_line (Second.Label & " = " & Trim (Second.Value'Image));

--  A component is assigned to like any other variable.
First.Value := 43;
put_line ("now " & Trim (First.Value'Image));

--  A whole record is copied by one assignment, and compared component by
--  component. Comparing only the first would call two different readings equal
--  whenever their labels matched, which is worse than refusing to compare.
Copy : Reading := First;

put_line ("the copy matches: " & Boolean'Image (Copy = First));
put_line ("and differs from the other: " & Boolean'Image (Copy /= Second));

--  An array holds many of one thing, reached by position. It may begin
--  anywhere, so `'First`, `'Last` and `'Length` are how a loop asks.
type Window is array (1 .. 4) of Integer;

Samples : Window := (12, 8, 30, 5);

put_line ("there are " & Trim (Samples'Length'Image) & " samples");

--  An array names its parts by index where a record names them by component,
--  and `others` answers for every part nothing else named. Which slot a named
--  part fills is decided where it is written, so the index is a value this
--  build knows before it runs.
--
--  What every form has in common is that each part gets exactly one value: a
--  part left out would be read as whatever its slot happened to hold, which is
--  what an aggregate exists to prevent. So a missing one, a repeated one, and
--  an `others` that answers for nothing are all refused.
Empty   : Window := (others => 0);
Ordered : Window := (4 => 40, 1 => 10, others => 0);
Paired  : Window := (1 .. 2 => 5, 3 .. 4 => 9);

put_line ("emptied to " & Trim (Empty (1)'Image)
          & ", and ordered to " & Trim (Ordered (1)'Image)
          & Ordered (2)'Image & Ordered (4)'Image);
put_line ("paired to" & Paired (1)'Image & Paired (2)'Image
          & Paired (3)'Image & Paired (4)'Image);

--  `Samples'Range` is what Ada defines it as -- `Samples'First ..
--  Samples'Last` -- so it stands wherever a range stands.
for Index in Samples'Range loop
   Put (Samples (Index)'Image);
end loop;

New_Line;

--  Indexing is written the way a call is written. Which one was meant is
--  settled by what the name denotes, not by the shape of the text.
Samples (2) := 99;

for Index in Samples'First .. Samples'Last loop
   put_line ("sample " & Trim (Index'Image) & " is "
             & Trim (Samples (Index)'Image));
end loop;

--  An index outside the array raises rather than handing back whatever the
--  next variable holds.

--  A slice is a run of the array's own elements, and one is assigned to
--  another: the copy is the block move a whole array's assignment is, from a
--  start moved along to the first element the slice covers. Its ends are known
--  before the program runs, as the array's own bounds are, because what a
--  slice becomes is a distance and a count of slots.
Recent : Window := (1, 2, 3, 4);

Recent (1 .. 2) := Samples (3 .. 4);

put_line ("the last two samples are" & Recent (1)'Image & Recent (2)'Image);
put_line ("and they are still there: "
          & Boolean'Image (Recent (1 .. 2) = Samples (3 .. 4)));

--  A part of a part, and an element of one. Each level is bounded by the level
--  outside it, and positions inside a part begin where a value of the type
--  begins -- one here, because Window is declared from one.
Recent (1 .. 3) (2 .. 3) := Samples (1 .. 2);

put_line ("the middle two are now" & Recent (2)'Image & Recent (3)'Image
          & ", and the second of the last three is"
          & Recent (2 .. 4) (2)'Image);

--  A composite travels to a subprogram as where its run of slots starts,
--  whatever mode it was declared with. An `out` parameter is how a program
--  hands one back.
--
--  A slice stands where the array stands for the same reason -- it is that
--  same kind of place, one level in. Its length has to be the parameter's own:
--  a parameter takes one length, there being no unconstrained array type here,
--  and a shorter slice would leave the callee reading slots that are not its.
function Largest (Of_Them : Window) return Integer is
   Best : Integer := Of_Them (Of_Them'First);
begin
   for Index in Of_Them'First .. Of_Them'Last loop
      if Of_Them (Index) > Best then
         Best := Of_Them (Index);
      end if;
   end loop;

   return Best;
end Largest;

put_line ("the largest is " & Trim (Largest (Samples)'Image));
put_line ("and of the whole of Recent, "
          & Trim (Largest (Recent (1 .. 4))'Image));

procedure Relabel (Which : out Reading; To : String) is
begin
   Which.Label := To;
end Relabel;

Relabel (First, "cpu");
put_line ("relabelled to " & First.Label);

--  What a composite is not: it holds simple values only. A record inside a
--  record, or an array of records, is refused -- reaching into one would need
--  an offset made of two offsets, and every place that walks a value would
--  have to recur.
--
--  A function does not return one either. A function's result is what it
--  leaves on the stack, and a composite is a run of slots rather than a value;
--  `Relabel` above is the shape that hands one back.
--
--  And a composite has no text form. `put_line` writes what a value is, and a
--  record is its parts: each of those has a text form where the whole has
--  none.
